REASONING=Main clause: "Luculli ... eum domum suam receperunt" — "receperunt" is the independent verb (transitive active), with "Luculli" as subject, "eum" as direct object, "domum" as accusative of place to which (bare accusative, so relationship 'accusative' -> receperunt), "suam" adjectival with "domum", and "statim" adverbial with "receperunt". Dependent clause: "cum praetextatus etiam tum Archias esset" — "cum" is a subordinating conjunction pointing to "receperunt"; "esset" is the dependent verb, a linking verb, with "Archias" as subject and "praetextatus" as predicate (adjective used as predicate complement of the linking verb, i.e. "still wearing the toga praetexta / still a boy"). "etiam" and "tum" are adverbs; "tum" modifies "esset" and "etiam" intensifies "tum" — since the only available label for adverbs points at the verb, both are given 'adverbial' -> "esset". (Minor judgment call: "praetextatus" could be taken as an attributive adjective with "Archias", but with a linking verb "esset" the predicate reading is clearly correct here.)

